  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nAside from continued river flooding in southeast NE, pretty \nquiet across the area weather-wise this evening. Surface ridging\nremained in control with the surface high centered over WI. To \nthe northwest, a shortwave trough was moving along the \nMT/Canadian border and will continue eastward through the day on\nSaturday. At the surface, a low will spin up and move through \nND or just along the Canadian border, eventually dragging a cold\nfront through the forecast area Saturday afternoon/evening. A \nfew CAMs do show some light simulated radar returns along the \nboundary as it moves through, but looking at associated \nsoundings, cloud bases will be pretty high with quite a bit of \ndry air below, so even if those returns pan out, not expecting \nmuch, if anything to reach the ground. We'll call it about a 5% \nchance of seeing anything. \n\nOtherwise, of note, temperatures today ended up about 5 to 9 \ndegrees warmer than model consensus, with dewpoints 5 to 10 \ndegrees lower, likely owing to some degree of bias correction \nwithin model blends. Therefore, bumped up temperatures a bit on \nSaturday and decreased dewpoints the next several days. While \nfire concerns remain fairly low with the recent rain, things \nwill start to dry out, especially as we start to see more \nsunshine and some breezier days into next week. Expect highs in \nthe 70s on Saturday, with mid 60s to lower 70s Sunday and \nMonday. Strong southerly low level flow returns on Tuesday, \nallowing temperatures to climb back into the 70s to perhaps \nlower 80s. A surface boundary will sag south into the area \nsometime Wednesday into Thursday, though temperatures will see \nlittle change through the remainder of the week. With a little \nmoisture in place and the boundary lingering in the area, not \ngoing to completely rule out a few pop up showers at times. \nHowever, with a lack of larger scale and stronger forcing for \nascent, and with a vast majority of guidance keeping us \ncompletely dry, chances will remain below 10%.",
